t names is gone.","targetDate":null,"progress":"0%"},{"id":"8cc60802-fd68-4837-90a9-2e5baacba433","name":"Product Decisions","description":"UI/UX tickets that are ready to build once one small product question is answered. Each ticket carries our proposed default, so Michael only has to say yes or no. Once a decision is made we write it down in the comments. If the ticket is approved we move it to V0 fixes; if it isn't, we close it and it stays in Product Decisions. If it turns out to need design or a new product rule, we move it to V1 fixes. Done when every ticket has a decision.","targetDate":null,"progress":"0%"},{"id":"cd521953-b1b7-41c5-9be7-b40c6fcc3782","name":"V0 fixes","description":"Bugs and small improvements found while testing V0 that need no decision: the problem and the fix are written in the ticket, so an engineer can finish them without asking anyone. Tickets sit in Backlog and we pull them into the weekly cycle, bugs first. The list is fixed: only tickets approved in Product Decisions join it, and bugs found later go to a new V0.2 fixes milestone. Done when every ticket is closed and the fixes are in a Roam Depot submission and an Obsidian BRAT release.","targetDate":null,"progress":"7%"},{"id":"b3ca13df-5364-4bdc-886c-9765b46770dd","name":"V1 fixes","description":"Work that needs design, a new product rule or a call from Architecture Decisions before anyone can build it, so it can't be settled with a one-line answer. Each ticket names the decision it waits on. Tickets sit in Triage. When we want to do one, it becomes its own project or a Product team feature ticket, and we link this ticket there and close it. The ones we don't want, we close. Done when no open tickets remain.","targetDate":null,"progress":"0%"}]}

